  "subject": "chemistry",
  "title": "Identifying organic and inorganic unknowns",
  "key_idea": "Test-tube tests identify functional groups: bromine water for alkenes, carbonate for acids, dichromate for oxidisable alcohols and aldehydes, and Tollens' reagent for aldehydes. Combine the results to identify an unknown.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Test-tube tests for organic groups",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A few quick tests on a small sample can show which functional group an unknown compound contains. This section covers the tests for alkenes, alcohols, aldehydes and carboxylic acids."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Group",
            "Test",
            "Positive result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Alkene",
              "Shake with bromine water",
              "Orange colour disappears (decolourises)"
            ],
            [
              "Carboxylic acid",
              "Add sodium carbonate solution",
              "Fizzing; CO₂ turns limewater milky"
            ],
            [
              "Primary or secondary alcohol, or aldehyde",
              "Warm with acidified potassium dichromate(VI)",
              "Orange changes to green"
            ],
            [
              "Aldehyde",
              "Warm with Tollens' reagent",
              "Silver mirror forms"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Tertiary alcohols and ketones do **not** change acidified dichromate, so it stays orange.",
            "Tollens' reagent separates an aldehyde from a ketone. Only the aldehyde is oxidised, and it reduces the silver ions to silver.",
            "The aldehyde is oxidised to a carboxylic acid, so the organic product of both oxidations is an acid."
          ]
        },
