        {
          "type": "key-points",
          "items": [
            "Learn each test as reagent, conditions and positive result.",
            "Dichromate turning green means a primary or secondary alcohol, or an aldehyde.",
            "Tollens' reagent picks out the aldehyde."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Identifying an unknown",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Use several tests together. One result rarely identifies a compound on its own."
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "items": [
            {
              "lead": "Test in a sensible order",
              "text": "Start with tests that use up little sample and are clear, such as bromine water and sodium carbonate."
            },
            {
              "lead": "Record every result",
              "text": "Write down the positive result or 'no change' for each test."
            },
            {
              "lead": "Narrow down",
              "text": "Rule out groups with a negative result and keep those with a positive one."
            },
            {
              "lead": "Confirm",
              "text": "Choose a final test that splits the groups left. For example, Tollens' reagent separates an aldehyde from a ketone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "A negative result is evidence too. A compound that does not decolourise bromine water has no C=C.",
            "Several groups may be present in one molecule, so more than one test can be positive."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "key-points",
          "items": [
            "Combine the tests and record every result.",
            "Use the negative results to rule groups out."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
