      "extra": [
        {
          "heading": "Detail this course asks for",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Three more groups are tested for, and the 2,4-DNP test has a wider use."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "items": [
                "**Haloalkanes**: warm with aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol. A precipitate of silver halide forms as the halide ion is released: white for chloride, cream for bromide, yellow for iodide.",
                "The precipitate forms faster with C–I than with C–Br or C–Cl, because the C–I bond is the weakest.",
                "**Phenols**: they are weakly acidic but do not react with carbonate, so there is no fizzing with Na₂CO₃. A carboxylic acid does fizz.",
                "**Carbonyl compounds** (aldehyde or ketone): add 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP). An orange precipitate forms. Tollens' reagent then separates an aldehyde from a ketone."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "aqa_alevel": {
      "spec_codes": [
        "AQA 3.3.6.1"
      ],
      "extra": [
        {
          "heading": "Detail this course asks for",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The practical tests for alcohols, aldehydes, alkenes and carboxylic acids are done in test tubes on a small scale."
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "items": [
                "Add a few drops of the unknown to each reagent in a separate tube. Label every tube.",
                "Warm the tubes for the dichromate and Tollens' tests in a beaker of hot water, not a flame.",
                "Use a fume cupboard where needed. Record the colour before and after for each test."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
